            <ArticleTitle>A Comprehensive Review of Central Post-Stroke Pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>804-18</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pmn.2015.03.002</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1524-9042(15)00032-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although central post-stroke pain is widely recognized as a severe chronic neuropathic pain condition, its consolidated definition, clinical characteristics, and diagnostic criteria have not been defined due to its clinically diverse features. The present study was undertaken to comprehensively review current literature and provide a more complete picture of central post-stroke pain with respect to its definition, prevalence, pathophysiology, clinical characteristics, and diagnostic problems, and to describe the range of therapies currently available. In particular, nursing care perspectives are addressed. It is hoped that this review will help nurses become knowledgeable about central post-stroke pain and provide valuable information for the drafting of effective nursing care plans that improve outcomes and quality of life for patients with central post-stroke pain.</AbstractText>
